= Computational complexity =

== Overview ==
'''Computational complexity''' studies how much computational resource is required to solve problems as input size grows.

Common resources include time, memory, communication, randomness, parallelism, and circuit size.

== Problems and algorithms ==
Complexity theory primarily classifies computational problems, while algorithm analysis studies the resource use of particular algorithms.

A problem can have many algorithms with different resource costs.

== Asymptotic growth ==
Resource use is commonly expressed as a function of input size n.

Asymptotic notation such as O(n), O(n log n), O(n²), or exponential bounds describes growth while abstracting away machine-specific constants.

This is not the same as measured wall-clock performance.

== Complexity classes ==
A complexity class groups problems solvable under specified resource bounds and computational models.

Important examples include P, NP, PSPACE, and EXPTIME.

Relations among classes can encode major open questions such as P versus NP.

== Worst, average, and parameterized views ==
Complexity can be studied under worst-case, average-case, amortized, smoothed, parameterized, or distribution-specific assumptions.

A worst-case bound does not directly predict every practical input.

== Computability and complexity ==
[[Computability]] asks whether an effective solution exists at all.

Complexity asks what resources solutions require.

Therefore:

<pre>
computable ≠ tractable
</pre>

== Related concepts ==
[[Algorithm]], [[Computability]], and [[Turing completeness]] distinguish resource classification from computability and universality.

